# TASK-1973 — a fixture is a byte contract, so its bytes must not depend on who
# checked it out.
#
# `src/adapters/companion/__fixtures__/fence-agreement.md` is checked in to BOTH
# this repo and choda-deck-companion, and TASK-1943's cross-repo test asserts the
# two implementations of `listMermaidFences` agree on every fence body derived
# from it. With `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default — git rewrote those 73
# line endings to CRLF on checkout. The parser then returned bodies carrying a
# trailing \r (deliberately: mermaid-check.ts keeps the slice VERBATIM so a caller
# rewriting a fence cannot silently convert the file to LF), and the pinned table,
# written with \n, no longer matched. Green on ubuntu, red on windows, for four
# commits.
#
# The parser is right and the test is right; the file was being mutated underneath
# both. Pinning eol=lf makes the fixture literally the same bytes everywhere,
# which is the only reading under which a "byte-for-byte identical in both
# repositories" contract means anything.
#
# Scoped to fixtures on purpose. A blanket `*.md eol=lf` would renormalise every
# document in docs/knowledge on the next checkout — a large diff, and none of
# those files are compared byte-for-byte by anything.
**/__fixtures__/*.md eol=lf
